{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he shall bring his guilt-offering to HaShem</b>. This offering is called אָשָׁם (guilt-offering), as it says, \"in the sacred shekel, for a guilt-offering.\" But the phrase \"and he shall bring his guilt-offering to HaShem\" stated above (Leviticus 5:6) concerning the graded offering — its meaning is \"and he shall bring his offering to HaShem,\" for that offering is a חַטָּאת (sin-offering), as it says (ibid.), \"a female from the flock, a lamb or a kid of the goats, for a sin-offering.\" And it has not been clarified what reason there is that the name of one offering should be \"חַטָּאת\" and the name of the other \"אָשָׁם,\" since all of them come for sin. And we cannot say it is because the חַטָּאת is female, for there is a male חַטָּאת — the he-goats and the bulls; nor can one say it is because of the severity of the sin, for behold the metzora brings two offerings, one called חַטָּאת and the second אָשָׁם.\n\nAnd what appears correct in my eyes is that the name \"אָשָׁם\" indicates a grave matter through which the one who commits it is liable to become desolate and perish in it — from the language of \"אֶשְׁמֵם אֱלֹהִים\" (Psalms 5:11), and \"וְיֶאֶשְׁמוּ נְאוֹת מִדְבָּר\" (they shall make the desert pastures desolate), and likewise \"תֶּאֶשַׁם שֹׁמְרוֹן כִּי מָרְתָה בֵּאלֹהֶיהָ\" (Hosea 14:1), and likewise \"אֲשֵׁמִים אֲנַחְנוּ\" (Genesis 42:21) — meaning punished. And חַטָּאת indicates a matter in which one deviated from the way — from the language of \"אֶל הַשַּׂעֲרָה וְלֹא יַחֲטִא\" (Judges 20:16). Now the guilt-offering for robbery and the guilt-offering for a betrothed maidservant are called \"אָשָׁם\" because they apply even to the intentional sinner, and likewise the guilt-offering of the Nazirite. But the guilt-offering for sacrilege (מְעִילוֹת), even though it is unwitting, is called \"אָשָׁם\" because it involves consecrated property of HaShem, for the grave sin makes him liable to become desolate in it — as it is called מְעִילָה (trespass). And the case of the metzora — because the metzora is accounted as dead, and behold he is already desolate and perished, his first offering is called \"אָשָׁם,\" to shield him from the desolation in which he lies desolate, and the second \"חַטָּאת,\" which atones for his unwitting sins.\n\nAnd the reason for the suspended guilt-offering (אָשָׁם תָּלוּי) is that its owner supposes there is no punishment upon him, for it was not known that he sinned. Because of this, Scripture dealt stringently with him in his uncertainty more than with his certainty and required a ram for two silver shekels, whereas had his sin been known he would have brought a חַטָּאת of a danka; and it called it \"אָשָׁם,\" meaning that it costs two selas like the severe guilt-offerings, to hint to him that if he treats it lightly in his eyes and does not bring his atonement, he will be held guilty in his iniquity. And this is the meaning of \"אָשָׁם הוּא אָשֹׁם אָשַׁם לַה'\" (Leviticus 5:19) — it says that this offering, even though it comes for a matter of doubt, is nonetheless an אָשָׁם, for \"guilty of guilt before HaShem\" who knows all hidden things, and if perhaps he did sin, He will punish him.\n\nAnd likewise what is stated above (Leviticus 5:6), \"and he shall bring his אָשָׁם\" — because among the cases mentioned there are intentional ones, such as the oath of testimony, it mentions אָשָׁם in reference to it, though it is a חַטָּאת.\n\nAnd by way of truth, the offering brought as a female for sin is called \"חַטָּאת,\" and the male is called \"אָשָׁם,\" and the he-goats and the bulls are also called \"חַטָּאת\" because they are for sin."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:39.759629049Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}